WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:08.820
Nearly every bit of data we use in our modern devices

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:10.580
has a price tag associated with it,

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:15.320
but there is one vital piece of technology that many of our devices depend on, that is free.

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:19.240
It allows our phones to give us turn-by-turn directions, no matter where we are in the world,

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:22.140
and it may one day even facilitate driverless cars.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.800
This, of course, is the Global Positioning System,

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:25.900
or GPS, for short.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:30.960
This satellite constellation costs upwards of 12 billion dollars to build and launch,

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:33.400
yet its service is available for free, worldwide.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:35.320
Well... not really for free.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:37.140
The US taxpayers paid for it,

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:39.340
and continue to pay approximately 2 million dollars a day,

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:41.120
to maintain and operate the system.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:43.720
But it wasn't always available for use.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:46.240
These satellites were launched for use by the military

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:51.920
to provide all divisions of the military with pinpoint accurate location information on their aircraft, ships,

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:54.720
and even their individual soldiers, anywhere on the planet.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.940
The first Global Positioning satellite, the Navstar 1,

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.360
was launched in February 22nd, 1978,

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:01.980
aboard an Atlas rocket.

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:04.980
Over the next 7 years, another ten GPS satellites

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:06.720
were successfully launched into orbit.

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:10.220
Only one of the twelve planned, Navstar 7, failed to launch.

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:13.640
These Block I satellites validated the system's capabilities

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:17.560
and the lessons learned from the project were carried forward to the Block II satellites,

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:20.120
which would complete the 24-satellite constellation,

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:23.460
providing worldwide GPS coverage for the US military.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:28.360
A 24-satellite constellation ensures that at least 4 satellites have a direct line of sight

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:31.180
with any point on the planet's surface at all times.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:35.340
This ensures the GPS constellation can pinpoint your location anywhere on Earth

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:36.980
through trilateration.

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:41.920
We know exactly where the satellites will be at any time as a result of predictable orbits,

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:46.280
and a ground station regularly checking in to make sure they are where we expect them to be.

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:49.740
GPS satellites constantly broadcast a radio signal

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:53.020
which the GPS receiver in your phone is constantly listening for.

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:57.000
This signal tells your phone the satellite's position, and what time it was sent at,

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.580
thus allowing the receiver to calculate how far away it is from the satellite,

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:03.820
as the radio signal travels at a known speed - the speed of light.

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:06.860
Each satellite tells us we are within X kilometres,

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:09.900
and thus we know we are somewhere on the circumference of a sphere

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:12.100
with a radius equal to that distance.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:17.320
Getting this information from 4 satellites allows us to pinpoint exactly where we are on Earth

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:20.280
by combining each sphere, and seeing where they intersect.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:23.920
Knowing the exact time is essential to making this system accurate.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.440
A divergence of 20 to 30 nanoseconds

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:29.460
would make the distance measurements unacceptably inaccurate,

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:31.880
so each satellite contains an atomic clock,

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:34.460
the most accurate timekeeping device available,

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:38.200
but, due to their size and cost, these cannot be used in phones

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:41.960
so the GPS satellites also constantly update the phone's time

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:46.440
to ensure it is able to calculate the distance between itself and the satellite accurately.

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:51.200
To keep this time measurement accurate, we even have to consider the effects of special relativity,

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:54.620
because the receiver on the ground is moving slower than the satellite in orbit,

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:57.240
which is travelling at 14,000 kilometres per hour.

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:01.740
Special relativity predicts that the onboard atomic clock will experience time slower

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:05.420
and thus fall behind the clocks on the ground by 7 microseconds a day.

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:10.500
Furthermore, the satellites are orbiting at 20,000 kilometres above the surface of the planet,

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:14.380
where the gravity of Earth has less effect on the curvature of spacetime.

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:19.860
General relativity tells us that the clocks closer to the mass of Earth will fall behind the ones in orbit,

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:23.440
so, if we calculate the time dilation due to gravity on the Earth's surface,

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:27.600
and then calculate the dilation at 20,000 kilometres from the planet's surface,

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:31.520
we can see that the clocks will diverge by 45 microseconds every day.

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:35.240
Combining these effects, we have to make the clocks onboard each satellite

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:38.560
to tick 38 microseconds faster than those on Earth.

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:41.060
If this effect wasn't taken into account,

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:44.620
GPS calculations would lose 10 kilometres of accuracy each day

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:46.360
making it completely useless,

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:50.520
but, thanks to the efforts of those ingenious engineers and Einstein's incredible discovery,

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:53.880
we have a Global Positioning System on our smartphones

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:56.180
with an accuracy within about 5 metres.

00:03:56.440 --> 00:04:00.760
But, as I said earlier, this system wasn't always available to the average citizen;

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:02.780
it was for military use only.

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:04.760
To open this system up to the public

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:07.960
would open a tool that could potentially be used against the US.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:11.220
After all, missiles used this system for navigation, too.

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:15.020
The incentive to open its use for civilian use came in 1983

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:18.480
when a Soviet Su-15 shot down a Korean passenger jet

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:21.880
that strayed from its intended route, into Soviet prohibited airspace.

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:25.860
An error that could've been avoided had the new form of navigation been available.

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:29.100
And so, on September 16th, 1983,

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:34.200
president Ronald Reagan announced the Global Positioning System would be made available for civilian use,

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:35.380
free of charge.

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:36.820
But, there was a catch:

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:40.500
the signal would be scrambled to decrease its accuracy to around 100 metres,

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:43.500
making it useful, but not by today's standards.

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:46.880
This was intended to keep the US military's tactical advantage,

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:49.660
and prevent the system from being used for nefarious purposes.

00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:53.020
It wasn't until 2000 that president Bill Clinton signed a bill

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:56.380
to stop the scrambling and open accurate GPS to all civilians,

00:04:56.380 --> 00:04:58.420
including those outside of America.

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:02.080
This was done in recognition of the huge economic benefits

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:04.600
GPS could provide to companies in America.

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:08.660
American armies have disrupted the signal in the past in areas like Iraq

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:12.820
by blasting out radio waves that disrupt the radio waves incoming from the satellites,

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:15.380
and this isn't a difficult thing to do.

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:18.360
In 2013, a truck driver in New Jersey

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:22.520
wanted to mask his GPS location from his boss and used a small jammer,

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:25.800
but inadvertently interrupted GPS navigation systems

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:28.260
in Newark Airport every time he drove by.

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:31.700
Beyond this simple jamming technique, which is easy to track,

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:35.380
the US could simply choose to take away this privilege at any point,

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:37.620
and cripple businesses across the world.

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:41.400
So, countries around the world have been building their own GPS systems.

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:44.140
Russia has its own 24-satellite constellation,

00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:46.360
and so does not depend on the US at all.

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:50.060
A similar system will be available for China and Europe in 2020,

00:05:50.420 --> 00:05:52.380
while Japan has 2 satellites in orbit

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:55.060
to increase GPS availability in its urban counties.

00:05:55.440 --> 00:06:00.000
These satellites' orbits have been tailored to ensure that one is directly overhead at all times.

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:04.500
Finally, India has 7 of its own satellites to cover India's land area alone.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:08.400
This tool is probably one of the most underrated results of the space program.

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:13.300
A simple radio signal that allows our farmers to operate driverless tractors to manage their land.

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:17.040
Commercial fishing vessels use it to navigate, and find fishing locations.

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:20.780
The aviation industry uses it for navigation, and collision avoidance.

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:23.240
Even drones would not be possible without it,

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:25.400
as anyone who has flown a DJI drone

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:28.600
knows that you have to have a connection to at least 6 satellites to fly.

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:32.700
Another technology that these drones have depended on to become so prevalent today,

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:34.440
is computational simulations,

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:37.620
and my friends at SimScale, who have kindly sponsored this video,

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:40.960
are working to make engineering simulations available to everyone.

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:44.720
The software I used for my thesis cost thousands of dollars to licence.

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:49.160
Even the engineering firm I was working for previously was unwilling to pay to use it,

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:54.040
but SimScale is available for free, if you don't mind your results being available to the public to view,

00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:57.260
which brings me to the next fantastic part of SimScale.

00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:01.580
You can view thousands of simulations from the community, and learn alongside them.

00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:05.640
You can even take an existing community simulation, and adapt it for your use.

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:09.600
They run workshops regularly to teach you how to design with their tools.

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:13.560
One of my favourites was a recently-run workshop on drone design.

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:16.260
I honestly can't say how much I love this platform.

00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:19.300
If you are a hobbyist, an engineer, or a future engineer,

00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:22.940
I really recommend you sign up, and try your hand at engineering simulation.

